WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.500 --> 00:00:05.300
Authentication design starts by identifying who or what can call the application.

2
00:00:05.400 --> 00:00:17.480
Human users, administrators, service accounts, machine clients, mobile apps, browser sessions, third-party integrations, webhook providers, and background workers are not interchangeable.

3
00:00:17.580 --> 00:00:23.740
Each identity type has a different trust boundary, credential type, lifecycle, and audit expectation.

4
00:00:23.840 --> 00:00:31.840
A customer login, admin dashboard, webhook receiver, and internal worker calling an API should not all be treated as the same flow.

5
00:00:32.120 --> 00:00:44.120
Map the trust boundaries between browsers, mobile apps, APIs, reverse proxies, identity providers, databases, caches, and external services.

6
00:00:44.220 --> 00:00:49.260
Decide which component authenticates the requester and which component consumes that result.

7
00:00:49.360 --> 00:01:03.680
For example, an identity provider may validate a user login, but the Node.js application still maps that external identity to a local account, checks account state, creates or validates a session, and enforces local authorization policy.

8
00:01:03.960 --> 00:01:10.760
Avoid spreading authentication decisions inconsistently across routes, controllers, middleware, and helper functions.

9
00:01:10.860 --> 00:01:20.060
The application should document the identity source, token issuer, session storage, credential type, account state checks, and failure behavior for each major flow.

10
00:01:20.160 --> 00:01:33.920
A simple boundary map helps reviewers ask useful questions before implementation: where is identity established, where is it trusted, what happens when it expires, and how does the rest of the request pipeline know validation succeeded?
